          ":question/problem": "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y|-y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|1|-1 & ={1}^{2}+C \\\\ 0-1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y|-y={x}^{2}-2\\,.\n$$"
